Subject and theorem
-------------------

The caller supplies an explicit exact 7-by-7 identity and four canonical
49-byte F11 matrices g0,...,g3. The kernel checks each full equation gi^2=I.
Thus every generator is invertible. Repeated or identity generators are
harmless to this theorem; any stronger Original-domain restrictions belong
to the operational wrapper. Multiplication must be the pinned, correct,
shared-bank F11 matrix implementation, never a vector-action or operator
substitute.

The carrier describes E0=I and n-1 nodes (p,j). Every parent is a strictly
earlier node and j is one of the four actual generator labels. The strict
consumer reconstructs Ei=Ep*gj. Induction proves every Ei is an actual word
in H=<g0,g1,g2,g3>. Earlier parents rule out cycles; no producer assertion
about a matrix is trusted. The consumer compares complete 49-byte matrices
and rejects any duplicate, including a later reconstruction of identity.

Next it checks Ei*gj belongs to the reconstructed set E for EVERY node i
and EVERY label j. Because I belongs to E, right closure implies every
positive generator word belongs to E. Every inverse generator equals that
generator, so every H-word belongs to E. Conversely the word trees proved
E is contained in H. Hence E=H. Distinct full matrices prove |H|=n exactly.
No subgroup recognition, group type, prime, seed, orbit, omitted-mark fact,
or ambient-group classification is used.

